ic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nH=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,K=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535473 17592186045443 true] [17592186162621 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}$ in terms of the components of $y.$" 13194139535473 17592186045443 false] [17592186162621 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}$ in terms of the components of $y.$" 13194139535473 17592186045443 true] [17592186162621 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngives the following expressions for ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving these for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(-{y}_{2})^{2}+(-{y}_{2}){y}_{1}-2{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}-2{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535473 17592186045443 false] [17592186162621 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end