llowing equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2} & =14 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt2$ and $\\sqrt{7}.$" 13194139535473 17592186045443 false] [17592186163231 1064 "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix with real entries. As we know, there exists an orthonormal change of variables \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nthat transforms the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}$ into the quadratic form $\\mathbf{y}^TD\\mathbf{y},$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix. \n\nThe columns of the matrix $P$ are the (normalized) unit eigenvectors of $A,$ which are mutually orthogonal. These are called the **principal axes** of the quadratic form.\n\nLet's use this knowledge to find out which curve is defined by the equation \n\n$$\n6x_1^2+4x_1x_2+3x_2^2= 14,\n$$\n\nand see the geometric interpretation of the principal axes.\n\nIf we plot our curve using a graphical calculator, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d8a6b6dff52e0c4004432f9447b5fd484612eaacf07588c3469af143b0a9e880.png)\n\nIt looks like an ellipse. Let's now find our coordinate system, in which the equation of the curve will be the simplest.\n\nFirst, we consider the corresponding quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})={\\color{blue}6}x_1^2+{\\color{red}4}x_1x_2+{\\color{blue}3}x_2^2,\n$$\n\nwhich we reduce to its canonical (diagonal) form. \n\nSo, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}6-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +14 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =7,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the principal axes of the curve.\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our quadratic form (and the corresponding curve) are\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that in the diagram below, the principal axes correspond to the axes $y_1$ and $y_2.$ \n\n![](images/10/10a2f0ccd179eba7f65410f932548d954a459e1bc8dcb0803a5b2d9fe7896432.png)\n\nIn the new coordinate system, our quadratic form can be reduced to \n\n$$\n7y_1^2 + 2y_2^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2} & =14 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt2$ and $\\sqrt{7}.$" 13194139535473 17592186045443 true] [17592186163233 1064 "We can compute a singular value decomposition (SVD) of any matrix! The algorithm for computing an SVD of a rectangular $m\\times n$ matrix $(m\\neq n)$ is similar to the algorithm for square matrices.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/f6/f65e0eee905da85ce0be4bbf39546c2b31c2bac082ff00a6a1d54fd5b89a4a4e.svg)\n\nNotice that $A$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix. So, to compute the SVD of $A,$ we must write\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{blue}{3}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}}$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.**  Find the singular values of $A.$ Computing the eigenvalues of $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 4, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1} =  2,  \\qquad \\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} =  \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T A$). Seeking non-zero solutions of $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_1 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ and $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_2 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvectors \n![](images/07/07085f021218ff17060b3c1a9099e1cabad24b3d12269f97f1fe50d49b970f6b.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A.$ Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the first two columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $U$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix. However, we do not have the third singular value, so we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n![](images/71/71f89dbcee33bce284b3085b872fdb9da2cc41166fe8a0bd26ef0bb5fe6876aa.svg) \nsuch that \n![](images/9e/9edc744372650e86c27f31ef15ac575cc8203803bdc5fe5730390727fd37146a.svg)\nSolving this system, we get the following nonzero normalized solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c2/c2bbd771d886da6b3a369b7e03345d1be75c18c26c66590598581d2d99590524.svg)" 13194139535473 17592186045443 false] [17592186163233 1064 "We can compute a singular value decomposition (SVD) of any matrix! The algorithm for computing an SVD of a rectangular $m\\times n$ matrix $(m\\neq n)$ is similar to the algorithm for square matrices.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/f6/f65e0eee905da85ce0be4bbf39546c2b31c2bac082ff00a6a1d54fd5b89a4a4e.svg)\n\nNotice that $A$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix. So, to compute the SVD of $A,$ we must write\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{blue}{3}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}}$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\b