o $\\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\n![](images/3a/3a1c79036954f0e04212892b7e3d79c75f8cd7b81d7785441f090f89a4eb6faf.png)\n\nA similar property holds for weighted least-squares solutions:\n\n*If the solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the least-squares problem $W\\!A\\mathbf x=W\\mathbf{b}$ is unique, then\n$A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ with respect to the weighted dot product defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle_W = \\sum_{i=1}^n w_i^2 x_i y_i,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the $w_i$'s are the weights from the main diagonal of the weight matrix $W.$* \n\nWe previously considered the weighted least-squares problem $W\\!Ax=W\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n![](images/d4/d44c48deb0864fb8fd6aa92ce8af408b600de8abf2039a3f3cd7397fabc7d9fe.svg)\n\nIt's easy to show (using the usual techniques) that the unique weighted least-squares solution is given by \n\n![](images/db/db8f62fcb085d8c1fcfc22bb53cb8f22bd9a65b211e5fd5d4017e30bf9bdb4c7.svg)\n\nComputing our error vector $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} = A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ε & =A\\hat{x}-b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ denote the columns of $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/3f/3fbafcf3f9d80d2d54089647b2f0239662dcbe56c9ba68539e2303ce752d28ee.svg)\n\nNotice that the error vector $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ is *not* orthogonal to the columns of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Computing $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} \\cdot \\mathbf a_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ε\\cdot {a}_{1} & =⟨\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =(1)(1)+(4)(1)+(-4)(2) \\\\  & =-3