\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} x_1 + \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} x_2 = 0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution ![](images/d1/d12d66468b003d9d3087537a078a80490e14905685ae1d97228a533833814168.svg) Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/a8/a86c404cfed6fbb9dbc477e2c095ea270ef56ab22069e1175b924877dd4c4c26.svg)" 13194139535473 17592186045443 true] [17592186166639 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}32 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=2\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=\\sigma_1^2=40,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-40I & =[\\begin{aligned}32 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 8\\end{aligned}]-40[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -16 \\\\ -16 & -32\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 40I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/56/56e07664fa7886a7076fa960dc7074e5ebd4aa76c90ead9034a34553b852d4f8.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=\\sigma_2^2=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}32 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 8\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}32 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 0I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/71