 {y}_{2}(a)={c}_{2}, \\\\ \\,\\,⋮ \\\\ {y}_{m}^{'}={f}_{m}(x,{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{m}),\\, & {y}_{m}(a)={c}_{m},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates a solution by applying the update rule component-wise. The increments are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{1}={y}_{1}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\ Δ{y}_{2}={y}_{2}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\ \\,\\,\\,⋮ \\\\ Δ{y}_{m}={y}_{m}^{'}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by $y_{i,\\text{new}} = y_i + \\Delta y_i,$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,m.$\n\nFor convenience, we can express large systems of ODEs in vector form. Let\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {y}_{m}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}' = \\mathbf{f}(x, \\mathbf{y}), \\qquad \\mathbf{y}(a) = \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nhas Euler update\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{y}' \\cdot \\Delta x\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{y}_\\text{new} = \\mathbf{y} + \\Delta \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nNext, we will apply this vector formulation to a specific numerical example." 13194139535473 17592186045443 false] [17592186168011 1064 "We can apply Euler's method to a system of *any number* of simultaneous first-order ODEs. That is, for an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{'}={f}_{1}(x,{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{m}),\\, & {y}_{1}(a)={c}_{1}, \\\\ {y}_{2}^{'}={f}_{2}(x,{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{m}),\\, & {y}_{2}(a)={c}_{2}, \\\\ \\,\\,⋮ \\\\ {y}_{m}^{'}={f}_{m}(x,{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{m}),\\, & {y}_{m}(a)={c}_{m},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates a solution by applying the update rule component-wise. The increments are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{1}={y}_{1}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\ Δ{y}_{2}={y}_{2}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\ \\,\\,\\,⋮ \\\\ Δ{y}_{m}={y}_{m}^{'}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by $y_{i,\\text{new}} = y_i + \\Delta y_i,$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,m.$\n\nFor convenience, we can express large systems of ODEs in vector form. Let\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {y}_{m}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}' = \\mathbf{f}(x, \\mathbf{y}), \\qquad \\mathbf{y}(a) = \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nhas Euler update\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{y}' \\cdot \\Delta x\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{y}_\\text{new} = \\mathbf{y} + \\Delta \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nNext, we will apply this vector formulation to a specific numerical example." 13194139535473 17592186045443 true] [17592186168064 1064 "We've already seen how to write the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf v\\in \\mathbb R^n$ relative some general basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\mathbb R^n.$ In this lesson, we will generalize this concept to abstract vector spaces.\n\nAs an example, consider the matrices $A_1$ and $A_2,$ given by\n\n![](images/28/2828c037a750f078b4d98b93248e800ec05d4899d7eb390dd135326314c44e70.svg)\n\nThe set $\\mathcal{B}=\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of the vector space $V = \\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}.$ Now, suppose that the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are \n\n![](images/2d/2db3ab1e4452eff2f19151cc87a805452433c7f305a80e5480c25241cba42c38.svg)\n\nLet's find the matrix $C$ using this information. \n\nAs usual, any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ can be written as\n\n$$\nx_1 A_1  + x_2 A_2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1,x_2$ are the coordinates of the vector relative to the basis. \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n![](images/64/6489e130a9237881664e1649cc5408cdff87cfb12dcf44c0342b9961cdf7bc81.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]+4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 12 \\\\ 8 & -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 10 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535473 17592186045443 false] [17592186168064 1064 "We've already seen how to write the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf v\\in \\mathbb R^n$ relative some general basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\mathbb R^n.$ In this lesson, we will generalize this concept to abstract vector spaces.\n\nAs an example, consider the matrices $A_1$ and $A_2,$ given by\n\n![](images/28/2828c037a750f078b4d98b93248e800ec05d4899d7eb390dd135326314c44e70.svg)\n\nThe set $\\mathcal{B}=\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of the vector space $V = \\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}.$ Now, suppose that the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are \n\n![](images/2d/2db3ab1e4452eff2f19151cc87a805452433c7f305a80e5480c25241cba42c38.svg)\n\nLet's find the matrix $C$ using this information. \n\nAs usual, any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ can be written as\n\n$$\nx_1 A_1  + x_2 A_2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1,x_2$ are the coordinates of the vector relative to the basis. \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n![](images/64/6489e130a9237881664e1649cc5408cdff87cfb12dcf44c0342b9961cdf7bc81.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 12 \\\\ 8 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 10 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535473 17592186045443 true] [17592186168070 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix, and \n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A.$ If the $i$th singular value of $A$ is nonzero, then the $i$th column of the orthogonal matrix $U$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nThe formula above works whenever $\\sigma_i\\neq 0.$ But how do we find the $i$th column of $U$ in cases where $\\sigma_i = 0?$ It turns out that we can find the corresponding left singular vector using the geometric properties of singular values.\n\nFor example, suppose that\n\n![](images/fa/fab8a223fc22100592865eeb8b147313f4a635ffa3791863826ca6330730cc58.svg)\n\nand $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ where\n![](images/58/58af793e95fd96171530b2b63e74d12e965fb02eb81af42c8fdb3c9289a04b11.svg)\nThe matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$ can be found using the methods we've seen before. So, our task now is to find the matrix $U.$ \n\nFrom $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ However, we know that $U$ is an orthogonal matrix. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n![](images/78/781532700996b7a60101f204b934483da264ecab6acd6bfeda97141669308b6c.svg)\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\|\\mathbf{u}_2\\|=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's consider each condition in turn:\n\n- The equation $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0$  is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{{2}} x_1 +  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} x_2 = 0.\n$$\n\nSeeking a nonzero solution to this equation, we get the vector \n![](images/75/7521799052487c5668d209c97ad6779f4258ecc5710ac81f2135966bf39974d3.svg)\n\n- Normalizing the vector we just found, we get\n![](images/30/30cc0b825690ed4dcd0bd672563f37bc6e621be092db70c4b46dafbf22db10c4.svg)\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal matrix $U$ is given by\n\n![](images/92/92ca53d48aed0c8435f89db4e11c022bc27ecb7a13d741e03270e0c981f639ef.svg)" 13194139535473 17592186045443 false] [17592186168070 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix, and \n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A.$ If the $i$th singular value of $A$ is nonzero, then the $i$th column of the orthogonal matrix $U$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nThe formula above works whenever $\\sigma_i\\neq 0.$ But how do we find the $i$th column of $U$ in cases where $\\sigma_i = 0?$ It turns out that we can find the corresponding left singular vector using the geometric properties of singular values.\n\nFor example, suppose that\n\n![](images/fa/fab8a223fc22100592865eeb8b147313f4a635ffa3791863826ca6330730cc58.svg)\n\nand $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ where\n![](images/58/58af793e95fd96171530b2b63e74d12e965fb02eb81af42c8fdb3c9289a04b11.svg)\nThe matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$ can be found using the methods we've seen before. So, our task now is to find the matrix $U.$ \n\nFrom $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ However, we know that $U$ is an orthogonal matrix. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n![](images/78/781532700996b7a60101f204b934483da264ecab6acd6bfeda97141669308b6c.svg)\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\|\\mathbf{u}_2\\|=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's consider each condition in turn:\n\n- The equation $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0$  is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{{2}} x_1 +  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} x_2 = 0.\n$$\n\nSeeking a nonzero solution to this equation, we get the vector \n![](images/75/7521799052487c5668d209c97ad6779f4258ecc5710ac81f2135966bf39974d3.svg)\n\n- Normalizing the vector we just found, we get\n![](images/30/30cc0b825690ed4dcd0bd672563f37bc6e621be092db70c4b46dafbf22db10c4.svg)\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal matrix $U$ is given by\n\n![](images/92/92ca53d48aed0c8435f89db4e11c022bc27ecb7a13d741e03270e0c981f639ef.svg)" 13194139535473 17592186045443 true] [17592186168073 1064 "It follows from the definitions that a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ has two repeated singular values $\\sigma_1= \\sigma_2= \\sigma$ if and only if the matrix  $A^TA$ has two repeated eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2 = \\sigma^2.$  Thus, since $A^TA$ is diagonalizable (because it is symmetric), the eigenspace corresponding to $\\sigma^2$ must be two-dimensional. Therefore, \n\n![](images/df/df266614e2576526353248439b7a17431d5e9e43153e1ba225adab3a89321373.svg)\n\nConsequently, we can use any pair of unit orthogonal vectors as a basis for the eigenspace corresponding to $\\sigma^2.$ \n\nLet's illustrate this with an example. Consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/ee/ee74c3d4ed7e32388d06b47ed79789d768b54dee61e64d6db57c3eeb582089ae.svg)\n\nLet's find a singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that since $A^T \\! A$ is diagonal, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = \\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$): Since the singular values are equal, we can always take as solutions of $(A^T\\!A-13I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ the unit eigenvectors \n![](images/d1/d1770843817b2557b032743e186dd27106374fef90a2a8e8bb3cfe77b2eb0398.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c9/c950cf3aff8f9ab47ede762fa9c4451c5e7fc1e78e12fca064ee87d89fcb571e.svg)\n\n**Note:** By swapping the order of eigenvectors in step 3, we swap the vectors $\\mathbf{u}_1$ and $\\mathbf{u}_2$ in step 4. However, the resulting matrices (with swapped columns) will give us a valid singular decomposition too." 13194139535473 17592186045443 false] [17592186168073 1064 "It follows from the definitions that a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ has two repeated singular values $\\sigma_1= \\sigma_2= \\sigma$ if and only if the matrix  $A^TA$ has two repeated eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2 = \\sigma^2.$  Thus, since $A^TA$ is diagonalizable (because it is symmetric), the eigenspace corresponding to $\\sigma^2$ must be two-dimensional. Therefore, \n\n![](images/